## ABSTRACT
Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) have achieved great success in the field of heterogeneous catalysis, however, it’s still challenging to design MOF catalysts with enhanced selectivity. Here, we demonstrated a combination strategy of metal design and ligand design on the enantioselectivity—that is the enantioselectivities of chiral MOF (CMOF) catalysts could be significantly enhanced by the rational choice of metal ions with higher electronegativities and introducing sterically demanding groups into the ligands. Four isostructural Ca-, Sr- and Zn-based CMOFs were prepared from enantiopure phosphono-carboxylate ligands of 1,1'-biphenol that are functionalized with 2,4,6-trimethyl- and 2,4,6-trifluoro-phenyl groups at the $^{3,3^{\prime}}$ -position. The uniformly distributed metal phosphonates along the channels could act as Lewis acids and catalyze the asymmetric transfer hydrogenation of heteroaromatic imines (benzoxazines and quinolines). Particularly, the Ca-based MOF 1 with 2,4,6-trimethyl groups at the substituents exhibited enhanced catalytic performance, affording the highest enantioselectivity (up to $97\%$ ). It is also the first report of the heterogeneous catalyst with chiral non-noble metal phosphonate active sites for asymmetric transfer hydrogenation reactions with Hantzsch ester as the hydrogen source. The catalyst design strategy demonstrated here is expected to develop new types of chiral materials for asymmetric catalysis and other chiral applications.
## KEYWORDS
metal–organic frameworks, metal phosphonate, Lewis acid, asymmetric catalysis, hydrogenation reaction
## Introduction
Heterogeneous catalysts are widely exploited in the industrial process due to their textural control and catalyst recovery [1, 2]. However, the nonuniformly distributed active sites and structural polydispersity severely limit the optimization of their catalytic activities and selectivities [2]. Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs), a novel class of porous crystalline materials with high chemical tunability, have attracted considerable attention in heterogeneous catalysis [3]. The presence of open metal sites makes MOFs highly promising Lewis acid catalysts [4]. Importantly, MOFs provide an ideal platform to enable finetuning of catalytic activities by the manipulation of organic linkers and inorganic nodes [5–11], allowing for the fine control over the relationship between structures and properties [7, 9, 12]. Currently, the majority of reported chiral MOFs (CMOFs) were built from the privileged chiral ligands [13–16], and some of them with Lewis acid active sites displayed moderate to excellent enantioselectivities in asymmetric catalysis [13–15, 17, 18]. Numerous strategies for enhancing the Lewis acidity of MOFs have been reported to date [7, 9, 19, 20]. However, only few strategies were devoted to CMOFs-based asymmetric catalysis [7, 21], and there is still a great demand for the development of CMOFs featuring high catalytic activity and selectivity.
Chiral phosphoric acids and their metal salts derived from axially biaryldiols such as BINOL and biphenol have been proven to be highly efficient for a wide variety of asymmetric transformations achieving exceptional enantiocontrol and high productivity as their skeletons can be easily modified [22, 23]. Generally, steric bulk at the $^{3,3^{\prime}}$ -positions of BINOL/biphenol is of high advantageous for the enantioselectivity, demonstrating a dual role in providing a bulky environment as well as tuning the electronics of the catalysis [22]. We have previously reported the synthesis of several Lewis acids-based CMOFs, and demonstrated effective strategies to enhance their catalytic activities and selectivities through the rational choice of metal ions or modifying the ligand structures [6, 7]. Thus, it can be concluded that the introduction of additional steric and electronic effects around the Lewis acidic metal phosphonate sites within the framework could provide a chiral porous microenvironment to give high degrees of enantio-induction. Encouraged by these results, herein we further evaluated the combination of the impact of the Lewis acidities of metal ions and the ligand steric hindrance in CMOFs on the catalytic performance, especially the enantioselectivity.
On the other hand, the direct hydrogenation of heteroaromatic imines provides an efficient and straightforward approach to access diverse chiral heterocyclic amine derivatives [24, 25]. Rueping reported the first metal-free reduction of quinoline derivatives by using 3,3-disubstituted binaphthyl-phosphoric acids as the catalysts in 2006 [26]. Subsequently, Shi established the organo-catalytic asymmetric transfer hydrogenation of quinolines and 1,4-benzoxazines by using SPINOL-derived phosphoric acid catalyst [27]. Gong demonstrated that chiral gold phosphate complexes of BINOL are highly enantioselective for the hydrogenation of quinolones [28]. Besides, incorporation of BINOL-based chiral phosphoric acids into microporous organic polymers and MOFs has led to effective heterogeneous catalytic systems as well [29–31]. Current homogeneous chiral metal catalysts for the imine hydrogenation were mainly based on noble metals including Au, Rh, Ru and Ir [28, 32–35], or their complexes of enantiopure phosphorus ligands that are often air-sensitive [35, 36]. It is thus highly desirable to develop more stable and recyclable solid catalysts from the most economical and environmentally friendly metals such as Ca.
In this work, we report the construction of four isostructural Ca-, Sr- or Zn-based CMOFs from two 1,1'-biphenol-derived phosphoric acid ligands functionalized with 2,4,6-trimethylor 2,4,6-trifluoro-phenyl at the $^{3,3^{\prime}}$ -position. For the first time, we showed that the CMOFs with non-noble metal phosphonates could act as Lewis acid catalysts for the asymmetric transfer hydrogenation of heteroaromatic imines (benzoxazines and quinolines). The combination strategy of ligand design and metal design on the enantioselectivity of MOF catalysts in asymmetric transfer hydrogenation reactions has also been demonstrated.
## 2 Results and discussion
## 2.1 Synthesis and characterization
The ligands $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathbf{L}^{1}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathbf{L}^{2}$ were prepared from $(R)$ - or $(S)\mathrm{-}6{,}6^{\prime}{-}$ dimethyl- $^{3,3^{\prime}}$ -di-tert-butyl- $^{5,5^{\prime}}$ -di (4-methoxycarbonyl)phenyl$^{1,1^{\prime}}$ -biphenyl- $^{2,2^{\prime}}$ -diol in six steps with $26\%$ and $31\%$ overall yields, respectively. Solvothermal reactions of $\mathrm{CaCl}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{Sr}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}$ or $Z n(N O_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ with $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathbf{L}^{1}$ or $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathbf{L}^{2}$ in a mixture of water, methanol and acetic acid (HOAc) at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ led to colorless rod-like crystals of $\mathrm{[Ca_{2}(L^{1})(H_{2}O)(M e O H)(O A c)]{\cdot}2H_{2}O}$ (1) $\mathrm{[Sr_{2}(L^{1})(H_{2}O)(M e O H)(O A c)]{\cdot}H_{2}O}$ (2), $[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathbf{L}^{1})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}]{\cdot}\mathrm{OAc}{\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ (3) and $\mathrm{[Ca_{2}(L^{2})(H_{2}O)(M e O H)(O A c)]{\cdot}H_{2}O}$ (4). The products were stable in air and insoluble in water and common organic solvents. They were formulated based on elemental analysis, IR spectra, and thermogravimetric analysis (TGA).
MOF 2 is isostructural to 1 and has a similar 3D network, containing 1D open channels in diameters of $1.31\ \mathrm{nm}\times1.31\ \mathrm{nm}$ (Fig. 1(e) and Fig. S3 in the ESM). Unlike 1 and 2, MOF 3 is a two-fold interpenetrated 3D network with one independent ${\bf L}^{1}$ and two independent $Z\mathrm{n}$ ions in the asymmetric unit (Fig. S5 in the ESM). The basic unit in 3 is a dinuclear $[Z\mathbf{n}_{2}]$ , the $Z\mathrm{n}$ ion in which adopts a distorted tetrahedral geometry by coordinating to one water molecule and three oxygen atoms from two bidentate carboxylate groups and one bidentate/bridging phosphonate group (Fig. 1(b) and Fig. S1(b) in the ESM). Each deprotonated ${{\bf{L}}^{1}}$ exhibits a tridentate coordination fashion, binding to three $[Z\mathbf{n}_{2}]$ units via two carboxylate groups and one phosphonate group, while each $[Z\mathsf{n}_{2}]$ unit connects with three deprotonated $\mathbf{L^{1}}$ , thus forming a 3D structure. The overall structure of MOF 3 contains two identical interpenetrating nets (Fig. S5(b) in the ESM). Moreover, the interpenetration of the 3D networks in MOF 3 does not affect aperture along $c$ -axis which possesses 1D open channels in diameters of $1.30\ \mathrm{nm}\times1.30\ \mathrm{nm}$ (Fig. 1(e) and Fig. S4 in the ESM). MOF 3 exhibits eta-type topology (Fig. 1(d) and Fig. S6(b) in the ESM). PLATON indicated that MOFs 1–3 have about $51\%$ , $52\%$ and $56\%$ void volume available for guest inclusion [37].
Despite needle-like crystals, the single-crystal X-ray diffraction data for MOF 4 was extremely weak. The unit cell parameter determination and powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) study showed that 4 is isostructural to 1–3 (Figs. 2(a) and 2(b), and Table S5 in the ESM). The phase purity of the bulk samples of 1–4 was established by comparison of their observed and simulated PXRD patterns. Circular dichroism (CD) spectra of 1–4 made from $R$ and S enantiomers of $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathbf{L}^{1}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathbf{L}^{2}$ are mirror images of each other, indicative of their enantiomeric nature (Fig. S9 in the ESM). PXRD patterns of the activated samples matched well with the pristine samples and simulated patterns from their crystal data (Figs. 2(a) and 2(b)), indicating that the frameworks remain intact after removal of guest solvent molecules. The permanent porosity was examined by $\Nu_{2}$ adsorption measurements at $77~\mathrm{K}.$ . They all exhibited the type I isotherms with a rapid nitrogen uptake at a low relative pressure $(<~0.1)$ , suggesting the microporous structures (Fig. 2(c)). The Brunauer–Emmett–Teller (BET) surface areas were calculated to be 1,163, 1,286, 931 and $545~\mathrm{m^{2}/g}$ for 1–4, respectively (Fig. S11 in the ESM).
## 2.2 Heterogeneous asymmetric catalysis
Enantiomerically pure heterocyclic amines have gained considerable attention from synthetic organic chemists [32]. Significant advancement has been made in the development of new strategies for the construction of chiral C–N skeletons [38], owing to their prevalence and importance in pharmaceuticals and agrochemicals [39, 40]. The presence of open channels and uniformly distributed metal phosphonate along the channels encourage us to evaluate their catalytic activities. All of them were found to be capable of catalyzing asymmetric transfer hydrogenation of benzoxazines and quinolines, but MOF 1 gave much higher stereoselectivity than MOFs 2–4.
We first investigated the performance of 1 in catalyzing asymmetric transfer hydrogenation reactions of benzoxazines [26, 27]. After screening the reaction conditions by varying the catalyst loading, solvents and temperature (Table S9 in the ESM), it was found that in the presence of $5\mathrm{mol}\%$ 1, (R)-3- phenyl-3,4-dihydro- $2H$ -benzo $[b][1,4]$ oxazinemethyl (7a) was obtained in $97\%$ yield and $90\%$ ee in $\mathrm{CHCl}_{3}$ at $40~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ after $10\mathrm{{h}}$ (Table 1, 7a). With the optimal conditions in hand, we expanded the reaction to a large scope of substrates. To our delight, the electronic properties and positions of substituents had a negligible impact on the enantioselectivities and yields. In all cases, the reactions gave the desired products in $92\%-98\%$ yields and $87\%-97\%$ ee (Table 1, 7a–7j). MOFs 2 and 3 with the same ligand but different metal ions could also catalyze the reactions under identical conditions, affording $92\%-98\%$ and $94\%-97\%$ yields with $68\%-77\%$ and $75\%-85\%$ ee, respectively (Table 1, $\mathbf{7a-}7\mathbf{d})$ . The ee values obtained with 2 and 3 were $13\%-26\%$ and $10\%-17\%$ lower than those of MOF 1. The observed differences in enantioselectivity match well with their absolute electronegativities: $\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}$ $(\sim31.39)$ , $\mathrm{Sr}^{2+}$ $(\sim27.3)$ and $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ $(\sim28.84)$ [41], which could be the reference to the Lewis acidities [42]. The order of the absolute electronegativities are
