Multiple experiments were performed to confirm the heterogeneity and recyclability of MOF 1. Firstly, after completion of the transfer hydrogenation reaction, MOF 1 could be recovered by simple filtration and reused at least 10 times without any loss of its activity and enantioselectivity (Fig. 2(d)). After 10 cycles, both the PXRD pattern and the $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ isotherm (BET surface area: $1,092\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g})$ of 1 remained almost the same as those of the pristine sample (Figs. 2(a) and 2(c), and Fig. S11 in the ESM). Secondly, a hot filtration test showed no catalysis by leached homogeneous species. Thirdly, the inductively coupled plasma optical emission spectrometry (ICP-OES) analysis of the reaction solution indicated almost no loss of $\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}$ ions $(<0.002\%)$ from the structure per cycle. Finally, to explore whether the catalysis by MOF 1 occurs at the external surface or within the channels, the more sterically demanding substrate 5k bearing bulky 3,5-dibenzyloxyphenyl group was subjected to the reaction (Table 1, 7k, Fig. S7 in the ESM). Only trace amount $(<5\%)$ of product was detected by MOF 1 even after $48\mathrm{{h}}$ , which was much lower than $72\%$ yield obtained with its homogeneous analogue. The extremely low yield suggests the size-selective nature of MOF catalyst, indicating that the catalysis occurs predominantly at the active sites within the inner channels.
