cavity for the next reaction. On the basis of the experimental results, MD simulation and literature precedents [27, 45–47], we propose a tentative mechanism for asymmetric transfer hydrogenation between 5a and b. As shown in Scheme S4 in the ESM, the active Ca site is generated after the activation prior to the catalysis, which further interacts with substrate 5a. In the intermediate state I, substrates 5a coordinates to the Ca site, in the meantime, substrate $\mathbf{b}$ forms a hydrogen bond with the phosphoryl oxygen atom. Thus both substrates 5a and b are activated and positioned closely. Subsequently, a hydride transfers from b to 5a through the proposed transition state, followed by the release of c and the formation of intermediate $\mathbf{II}$ , in the intermediate state $\mathbf{II},$ , the formed $(R)$ -product coordinates with the $\mathrm{Ca}$ site by its oxygen atom, which further undergoes the release of the final $(R)$ -product and the regeneration of active Ca site (Fig. 3 and Scheme S4 in the ESM).
## 3 Conclusion
We have demonstrated that the enantioselectivities of MOF catalysts could be readily tuned by changing the electronegativities of metal ions and modifying the steric hindrance of ligands of MOFs. The Ca-based MOF 1 bearing 2,4,6-trimethylphenyl groups exhibited enhanced catalytic enantioselectivities in the asymmetric transfer hydrogenation of heteroaromatic imines. Moreover, MOF 1 could be recycled at least 10 times without any loss of its activity and enantioselectivity. The MD simulations allow us to gain more insight into the interaction between active Lewis acid sites and substrates. This work may provide a new strategy to design CMOF catalysts, especially with non-noble metal ions, with enhanced catalytic performances. We believe that the concepts described here could lead to new types of chiral materials for asymmetric catalysis, chiral separation and other applications.
